  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery currently shows one upper impulse lifting \nacross the Upper Mississippi Valley with a much stronger \nshortwave trough digging from the Pacific Northwest into \nNorthern CA. Meanwhile, surface high pressure remains over the \nMid-Atlantic States with a weak stationary front extending from \neastern Nebraska into northwest KS. Fog has started to develop \nover western KS and will continue to expand early this morning. \nWhile the more widespread dense fog will remain west of our \nforecast area, there is a chance at least some patchy fog could \naffect areas along and west of Highway 14 this morning. \n\nWestern CONUS upper trough is expected to lift out in a couple \ndifferent pieces. The lead piece of energy will lift out of the \nGreat Basin and into the Northern/Central Plains by Fri afternoon. \nMeanwhile, the more robust vort lobe will linger back across the \nDesert Southwest on Fri night. Still looking for lee troughing the \nstrengthen on Fri as the lead wave lifts out. This will bring strong \nsouth winds to the area, with the highest speeds northwest of the KS \nTurnpike. Current thinking is that we are looking at speeds a bit \nhigher than what we had Wed, with gusts in the 45-50 mph range \npossible. Not out of the question that a wind advisory may \neventually be needed. \n\nSouthern stream impulse is expected to track across NM on Sat and \nacross OK/KS on Sat night. Scattered showers are expected Sat \nafternoon ahead of the wave as 850-700mb moisture transport \nincreases and will become more numerous Sat night as the vort lobe \nand cold front move through. There also looks to be enough \ninstability for a few storms to mix-in on Saturday evening with \nstrong or severe storms not expected. To start the work week the \nPlains will get back into some zonal flow with a low amplitude wave \nset to track over the Rockies on Mon night. The bad news is that \nbeyond Mon, model solutions are all over the place with none of\nthem showing much consistency. The ECMWF digs more of the \nenergy further south while the GFS remains more phased and holds\nthe trough further north. \n\nHigh confidence remains in above normal temps for today through Sat \nwith upper 60s to around 70 likely. Normal highs for this time of \nyear are in the middle 50s. Just took a quick look at records and it \ndoesn't look like any will be threatened over the next few days.\nCooler temps will move-in behind the front for Sun, knocking \nhighs back down into the 50s for Sunday and Monday.",
